1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,968
Saturn is losing its rings.

2
00:00:01,968 --> 00:00:02,642
Right now.

3
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:06,010
The rings are about ninety-eight percent water ice,

4
00:00:06,010 --> 00:00:09,038
and bits of that ice are raining down into the planet.

5
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:13,933
NASA says it's enough to fill an Olympic swimming pool every half hour.

6
00:00:13,933 --> 00:00:15,167
In its final orbits,

7
00:00:15,167 --> 00:00:20,167
the Cassini spacecraft measured up to ten thousand kilograms a second falling in.

8
00:00:20,683 --> 00:00:21,416
At that rate,

9
00:00:21,416 --> 00:00:25,418
NASA says the rings could be gone in less than a hundred million years.

10
00:00:25,917 --> 00:00:27,561
They might be young, too.

11
00:00:27,561 --> 00:00:31,310
Cassini data suggests ten to a hundred million years old.

12
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:35,389
But a 2024 study argues they could be as old as Saturn itself.

13
00:00:35,883 --> 00:00:37,808
Either way, we're lucky to see them.

14
00:00:37,808 --> 00:00:39,091
What if Earth had rings?

15
00:00:39,091 --> 00:00:40,480
Full story on the channel.
